{
  "Study.Name": "Longitudinal Recovery Dynamics and Contextual Processes Study",
  "authors": "Dr. Sarah K. Johnson, Dr. Michael Chen, Dr. Lisa Rodriguez, Dr. James Patterson",
  "funding": "NIH National Institute on Drug Abuse Grant R01DA045678; Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration Award TI123456",
  "abstract": "This intensive longitudinal study examines the daily micro-processes of recovery from substance use disorders through ecological momentary assessment (EMA). Participants (N=300) in early recovery complete brief surveys 10 times daily for 28 days, capturing real-time fluctuations in craving, affect, stress, coping behaviors, social context, and substance use. A comprehensive baseline assessment characterizes demographic, socioeconomic, and clinical history variables. The study aims to identify dynamic, context-dependent predictors of lapse and sustained abstinence, informing the development of just-in-time adaptive interventions for substance use disorders.",
